course: biology
lesson: 187
title: Record-breakers: the loudest and the longest-lived
level: primary-4
stars: 2
explain: The loudest animal is not a lion but a sperm whale clicking in the deep sea, and one of the noisiest is a shrimp no longer than your finger that snaps its claw with a bang like a cap gun.
explain: Some animals and plants live astonishingly long: a clam from Iceland lived about five hundred years, older than Shakespeare, and bristlecone pines on a California mountain have been growing for nearly five thousand years.
explain: Scientists find these ages by counting rings, like the rings in a tree stump or on a clam's shell, one ring for each year of growth.
explain: As a rule small, fast-living animals such as mice live only a few years and big, slow ones live long, but there are surprises, like a little rodent that lives over thirty years and a jellyfish that can turn young again.
fact: The sperm whale makes the loudest sound of any animal; its clicks are made in its huge head, focused like a beam, and could hurt a diver very close by.
fact: The pistol shrimp snaps its claw so fast that a bubble forms and bursts with a loud crack that stuns small fish, and for an instant the bubble is nearly as hot as the surface of the Sun.
fact: Cicadas are among the loudest insects; males click drum-like plates on their bodies, and some American cicadas spend thirteen or seventeen years underground before coming out together.
fact: A clam from Iceland named Ming lived about five hundred and seven years, worked out by counting the growth rings on its shell.
fact: Bristlecone pines grow very slowly in harsh mountain places and can live for nearly five thousand years; one called Methuselah is over four thousand years old.
fact: The immortal jellyfish can turn back into a young polyp (a tiny baby stage stuck to a rock) when it is hurt or old, so in theory it could go on forever, though most are eaten.
fact: Some deep-sea glass sponges, with skeletons of glass-like material, may live for thousands of years in the cold, slow-growing deep.
fact: Jonathan, a giant tortoise on St Helena who hatched around 1832, is thought to be the oldest known living land animal.
fact: Big parrots such as macaws and cockatoos can live over sixty years, so a pet parrot may outlive its owner.
fact: Small animals such as mice use energy fast, have many young quickly and live only a few years, but the naked mole-rat lives over thirty years.
fact: The coral reef pygmy goby lives about eight weeks, one of the shortest lives of any animal with a backbone, and some adult mayflies live only a few hours.
exercise: Which animal makes the loudest sound of any animal? | The sperm whale | The African lion | The African elephant | The howler monkey
exercise: Where are a sperm whale's clicks made? | In its huge head | In its long tail fin | In its big stomach | In its two flippers
exercise: How does a pistol shrimp make its bang? | It snaps its claw so fast a bubble forms and bursts | It hits a rock hard with its big tail to make a loud bang | It shouts through its mouth | It claps two shells together
exercise: What does the pistol shrimp's bang do to small fish? | Stuns them | Feeds them up | Makes them grow | Calls them to play
exercise: How do cicadas make their loud sound? | Males click drum-like plates on their bodies | They rub their eyes | They whistle loudly through their beaks like birds | They sing with their mouths
exercise: How long do some American cicadas stay underground? | Thirteen or seventeen years | Thirteen or seventeen days only | One hour | All their lives
exercise: How old was the clam named Ming? | About five hundred and seven years | About fifty years | About five years | About five thousand years or even more
exercise: How did scientists find the age of Ming the clam? | By counting growth rings on its shell | By asking the old fishermen who caught it | By weighing it | By counting its teeth
exercise: How old can bristlecone pines become? | Nearly five thousand years | About fifty years | About five hundred days in all | A million years
exercise: Why can bristlecone pines live so long? | They grow very slowly in harsh mountain places | They are watered every single day by the park rangers | They grow in warm jungles | They are made of stone
exercise: What can the immortal jellyfish do when it is hurt or old? | Turn back into a young polyp | Turn into a small fish and swim off | Grow wings and fly over the waves | Freeze solid and wake up much later
exercise: How long may some deep-sea glass sponges live? | Thousands of years | A few weeks | Exactly ten years | One day
exercise: Who is Jonathan? | A giant tortoise on St Helena, thought to be the oldest known living land animal | A sperm whale in the Arctic | A famous parrot in London | The clam from Iceland
exercise: How long can big parrots like macaws live? | Over sixty years | Only about six years | Only about six months | Over six hundred years
exercise: Why do mice live only a few years? | They use energy fast and have many young quickly | They never sleep | They eat far too much cheese and get sick from it | Cats always catch them
exercise: Which rodent lives over thirty years? | The naked mole-rat | The common house mouse | The pet golden hamster | The little desert gerbil
exercise: Which animal with a backbone lives only about eight weeks? | The coral reef pygmy goby | The giant tortoise of St Helena | The macaw | The sperm whale
test: «Jerry from Tom and Jerry is like 80 years old, mice live forever lol» What is right? | Real house mice live only a few years | Right, mice can live for many centuries | Mice live exactly eighty years | Mice never get old
test: «grandad got his parrot when he was a kid, it's still alive, impossible» What is right? | Big parrots can live over sixty years | Right, parrots only live for about two years | It must be a different parrot | Parrots never get old
test: «Jonathan hatched around 1832, it's 2026 now, about how old» | About 194 years | About 94 years | About 294 years | About 32 years
test: «3 cicadas buzzing and 2 mice, how many legs» | 26 | 20 | 24 | 30
test: «loudest animal is the lion obviously» What is right? | The sperm whale makes the loudest sound of any animal | Right, nothing is louder | The tiny house mouse is the loudest, squeaking in the walls | Fish make no sound at all
test: In SpongeBob, Mr Krabs pinches with his claws. Which real crustacean stuns fish with a bang from its claw? | The pistol shrimp | The hermit crab | The lobster at dinner | The krill
test: «the immortal jellyfish can't ever die» What is right? | It can turn young again, but most are eaten | Right, nothing in the whole sea can ever kill it | It dies after one day | It is a robot
test: What would happen if a farmer counted the rings on a bristlecone stump? | He could tell roughly how many years it grew | He would learn its colour | He would learn how tall it was when it was cut | Nothing, trees have no rings
test: «a fish that lives 8 weeks?? that's shorter than summer holidays» What is right? | Yes, the coral reef pygmy goby lives about eight weeks | No fish lives for less than a year, not even the tiniest | Fish live forever | It is a mayfly, not a fish
test: «cicadas just appeared everywhere this year, where were they hiding» | Underground for thirteen or seventeen years | In the clouds | In other countries | Inside trees as seeds waiting for the spring rain
test: Which is usually true of animal lives? | Small, fast-living animals live short lives and big ones longer, with exceptions | Small animals always live longest | All animals live the same time | Big animals always die first
exam: Identify the animal which produces the most intense sound known in the animal kingdom. | The sperm whale | The African lion | The howler monkey | The cicada
exam: Ascertain the method by which the age of the clam known as Ming was determined. | The enumeration of annual growth rings upon its shell | The examination of its teeth | Its weight in comparison with younger clams | Records maintained by fishermen
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the immortal jellyfish? | It is capable of reverting to a juvenile polyp when injured or aged | It is immune to all predators | It survives for one day only | It transforms into a fish upon maturity
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the lifespan of small mammals. | Most live only a few years, although the naked mole-rat exceeds thirty | All survive for several centuries | All small mammals live precisely ten years | The house mouse is the longest-lived of rodents
